# Project: Hosted AI Voice-to-Voice Companion (cs576 group 10)

## Goal
An AI voice-to-voice companion. No local models: every AI step (speech-to-text, chat, embeddings) runs on a hosted API. ElevenLabs provides TTS. Avatar/lipsync rendering is out of scope for now (see TBD).

## Character
**Frieren** (Frieren: Beyond Journey's End) — canon-faithful, deadpan elf mage traveling companion who serves as the user's mage assistant. Persona prompt lives in `server/soul`. Helping the user is framed as casting spells; those spells are intended to map to agentic tool/function calls later. WebSocket `who` value is `"frieren"`. Client labels use Frieren / Grimoire. Art is `client/public/images/frieren/pixFrieren.gif` (animated while speaking); the still frame for the title bar, taskbar, and idle portrait is generated in the browser from the GIF's first frame. Desktop chrome lives in `client/public/images/background/`.

## Stack decisions

Do not re-litigate these choices unless there's a real problem. The OpenAI migration (STT, chat, embeddings, summary) is implemented; one `OPENAI_API_KEY` covers all OpenAI calls via `server/openai_client.py`.

- **Speech-to-text**: OpenAI `STT_MODEL` (default `gpt-4o-mini-transcribe`; `whisper-1` also works) via the `openai` SDK. Each utterance is uploaded as an in-memory WAV. Turn-taking is the RMS energy-threshold VAD in `speechToText.py` (not a native VAD library — `webrtcvad` has no Python 3.14 wheels and is unmaintained). `faster-whisper` and the CUDA DLL helpers are removed.

- **Chat generation**: OpenAI `gpt-5.6-luna` with reasoning off (`reasoning_effort="none"`, set by `LLM_REASONING_EFFORT`, default `none`) via the `openai` SDK in `local_model.py`. Reasoning tokens would count against `max_completion_tokens` and empty out the short replies. Env: `OPENAI_API_KEY`, `LLM_MODEL` (required), optional `LLM_REASONING_EFFORT`. Length presets set `max_completion_tokens`. `LocalModel.start()` verifies the key and model; `.ask()` sends the turn; `ask_stream()` (N3b) yields SSE deltas; `complete_chat()` is the shared helper. Note: OpenAI moderates output, which may change persona behavior versus the previous uncensored local model.

- **Embeddings**: OpenAI `text-embedding-3-small` with `dimensions=768`, so the Supabase `vector(768)` column and `match_memory` RPC stay unchanged.

- **Rolling summary**: the summarizer in `chat_memory.py` uses the same `complete_chat()` helper as replies.

- **TTS**: ElevenLabs REST `text-to-speech` in `server/elevenlabs_tts.py` (character replies only). Env: `ELEVENLABS_API_KEY`, `ELEVENLABS_VOICE_ID` in `server/.env`. Uses one pooled `httpx` connection (`server/http_pool.py`, opened at startup by `warm_up()`). By default (N3) TTS uses the `/stream` endpoint (`synthesize_stream`) and MP3 chunks are forwarded as they arrive (`audio_start` / binary chunks / `audio_end`), played progressively via MediaSource in the React client; with `LLM_STREAMING=1` the reply text is streamed too (`local_model.ask_stream`, split by `server/sentence_stream.py`) and each sentence gets its own TTS request (`previous_text` = what was already spoken) inside one audio stream. `TTS_STREAMING=0` restores the N2 full clip (`audio` JSON header plus one binary frame).

- **Bridge**: custom orchestrator / harness (Python) that clients connect to via WebSocket. Chosen over a folder-watch approach so the connection can carry more than audio later (expressions, status, interrupts).

- **Chat memory (Supabase)**: four-tier schema in `supabase/schema.sql` (run once per new project) — `key_facts`, `rolling_summary`, `chat_memory` (`vector(768)` + HNSW), `recent_turns`, plus RPC `match_memory`. API in `chat_memory.py` (`store_turn`, `should_retrieve_memory`, `build_light_context` / `build_full_context`, `build_context_if_needed`, `prune_recent_turns`, etc.). Selective retrieval: heuristic gate skips semantic search on filler/chatty turns; light context = facts + summary (session-cached) + recent turns; full context adds `retrieve_relevant`. Wired into `voice_chat.py`: `build_context_if_needed` each turn (logs `(memory: light|full)`), turns stored after each reply, prune every 10 assistant turns (invalidates light cache). Env: `SUPABASE_URL` (project root, no `/rest/v1`), `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY`.

## Architecture
```
Mic → RMS VAD → OpenAI STT → text
→ OpenAI chat (+ Supabase memory context) → text reply (streamed per sentence with LLM_STREAMING)
→ ElevenLabs streaming TTS → MP3 chunks
→ WebSocket broadcast (chat UI bridge, localhost:8766), chunk by chunk
→ React client (VN textbox + MediaSource MP3 playback)
```

## Message protocol (WebSocket, JSON)
Chat UI bridge (live, localhost:8766 via `server/chat_bridge.py`, started by `voice_chat.py`):
Server → client:
```json
{"type": "chat", "who": "you", "text": "..."}
{"type": "chat", "who": "frieren", "text": "..."}
{"type": "audio_start", "format": "mp3", "stream": 3}     (N3 default)
<binary frame: one MP3 chunk, repeated as chunks arrive from ElevenLabs>
{"type": "audio_end", "stream": 3, "bytes": 76947, "chunks": 61}
{"type": "audio", "format": "mp3", "encoding": "binary", "bytes": 76947}   (TTS_STREAMING=0)
<binary frame: the 76947 MP3 bytes>
{"type": "audio", "format": "mp3", "data": "<base64>"}   (legacy, WS_AUDIO_BINARY=0)
{"type": "status", "value": "listening"}
{"type": "status", "value": "thinking"}
{"type": "status", "value": "speaking"}
{"type": "status", "value": "ready"}
{"type": "status", "value": "error", "detail": "..."}
```
Client → server:
```json
{"type": "playback_started", "recv_ts": 1763420000000, "play_ts": 1763420000045, "stream": 3}
{"type": "playback_done", "stream": 3}
```
`playback_started` (N1) carries browser `Date.now()` timestamps for when the audio (first chunk, for streams) arrived and when sound started (`"blocked": true` if autoplay held it until a click). Stream reports carry the stream id; while a stream is current the server ignores untagged reports (a stale second tab once unmuted the mic mid-reply). Without MP3 MediaSource support the client plays the collected chunks as one clip at `audio_end`. `voice_chat.py` combines them with server stage marks (`server/metrics.py`) into one CSV row per turn in `findings/data/`; `server/metrics_summary.py` prints median / p90 per stage.
**Half-duplex turn-taking**: after emitting `audio` / `audio_end`, `voice_chat.py` waits (`chat_bridge.wait_for_playback`) for `playback_done` before listening again, then flushes queued mic frames so Frieren's voice from the speakers isn't transcribed as the user. Fallback timeout = estimated clip length (128 kbps MP3) + 3 s; no wait when no UI is connected. The user cannot interrupt (barge-in) while she speaks.

Errors: the client keeps the last error visible in the status bar until the next user turn.
The Vite client proxies `/ws` → `ws://127.0.0.1:8766`, live-replaces the VN textbox on each `chat` event, and plays character audio via MediaSource (`audio/mpeg`, sequence mode) with a Blob-at-`audio_end` fallback when MSE MP3 is unsupported. Mic capture remains in Python; STT/TTS run via hosted APIs.

Every future feature should be a new `type` value, not a new connection or transport.

## Networking focus (cs576)

### Already in the project (point to these)
- **Multi-service pipeline over HTTPS**: each turn makes TLS REST calls to OpenAI (STT multipart upload, chat, sometimes embeddings), Supabase (PostgREST over HTTPS, `match_memory` RPC), and ElevenLabs, then pushes results over a WebSocket.
- **WebSocket upgrade + bidirectional protocol**: HTTP `101 Switching Protocols` handshake, JSON message types in both directions (server `chat`/`audio`/`status`, client `playback_done`). `websockets` sends keepalive pings every 20 s by default.
- **Reverse proxy**: Vite proxies `/ws` → `ws://127.0.0.1:8766` (same-origin for the browser; server bound to loopback only).
- **Reconnect with exponential backoff**: client retries ×1.5 per attempt, capped at 10 s.
- **Application-level flow control**: the half-duplex `playback_done` handshake with timeout fallback (see Message protocol).
- **N2 changes (before/after in `findings/N2-...md`)**: the before capture showed `permessage-deflate` already squeezed base64 audio back to ~MP3 size on the wire, and every turn opened a new TLS connection to OpenAI, Supabase, and ElevenLabs (httpx's default 5 s `keepalive_expiry`; `urllib` for ElevenLabs), about 170 ms per turn. Now: binary audio frames with WS compression off (`WS_AUDIO_BINARY`, `WS_COMPRESSION` toggles in `chat_bridge.py`), and all three API clients share keep-alive settings from `server/http_pool.py` (`HTTP_KEEPALIVE_SECONDS`, default 300). CSV gains `ws_bytes` and `tts_handshake_ms`.
- **N3 changes (before/after in `findings/N3-streaming-tts.md`)**: `TTS_STREAMING` (default 1) streams TTS chunks as `audio_start` / binary frames / `audio_end`. `LLM_STREAMING` (default 1) streams the reply and synthesizes per sentence (`sentence_stream.py`) into one audio stream. CSV gains `tts_ttfb_ms`, `chunks`, `first_chunk_bytes`, `llm_ttft_ms`, `first_sentence_ms`, `sentences`.

### Planned additions (priority order)
N = networking feature, numbered by priority. Every item should produce a before/after number for the report; N1 is the measuring tool for the rest.

| # | Item | Effort | What to measure / show |
|---|---|---|---|
| N1 | **Latency instrumentation**: timestamp each stage (end of speech → STT RTT → LLM → TTS → WS delivery → playback start); WS ping/RTT; a `metrics` message type and a small network panel in the UI; CSV log per turn | Low | Per-stage latency breakdown; total voice-to-voice latency |
| N2 | **Binary WS frames + connection reuse**: send MP3 as binary frames instead of base64 JSON; keep-alive HTTP sessions for ElevenLabs, OpenAI, and Supabase | Low | Handshakes per turn and time saved; payload bytes (on-the-wire saving is ~0 because deflate already removed base64 overhead) |
| N3 | **Streaming audio**: ElevenLabs chunked HTTP + optional LLM text streaming / per-sentence TTS; client MediaSource (Blob fallback) | Medium | Time-to-first-audio before vs after (N3a then N3b) |
| N4 | **Browser mic → server audio streaming**: capture in the browser, stream 16 kHz/16-bit PCM (256 kbps) as binary WS frames; server VAD/STT on the stream. Makes it true client-server; phone or another LAN device can be the client | High | Bandwidth, chunk size vs latency, jitter, backpressure |
| N5 | **Network-condition experiments**: clumsy (Windows) or Chrome DevTools throttling to add latency, loss, bandwidth caps | Low (after N1) | How each stage degrades; TCP head-of-line blocking under loss |
| N6 | **Security + multi-user**: `wss://` via reverse proxy, auth token checked on the WS handshake, `Origin` check against cross-site WebSocket hijacking, per-user sessions (also fixes shared memory) | Medium | Threat model; handshake trace |
| N7 | **Resilience**: tuned heartbeats, session resume with message sequence numbers, retry with jittered backoff on API errors / HTTP 429 | Low | Recovery time after a dropped connection |
| N8 | **Protocol comparison (stretch)**: WebSocket/TCP audio vs WebRTC (UDP + Opus), e.g. OpenAI Realtime over WebRTC as a baseline | High | Latency and loss tolerance, TCP vs UDP |

**Recommended scope**: core = N1 + N2 + N3; then N4 with N5 as the experiments section if time allows. Capture the WS handshake and frames in **Wireshark** for the report (loopback capture via Npcap).

**Findings**: results go in `findings/`, one file per item (`N1-…md` to `N8-…md`, plus `00-baseline-e2e.md`, `wireshark-captures.md`, `decisions-log.md`). CSV logs go in `findings/data/` and captures in `findings/captures/`. See `findings/README.md` for conventions (n ≥ 10 turns, median + p90, same prompt script).

## Known unresolved items / caveats
- Package installs require explicit user confirmation (workspace rule).
- Default TTS path streams (`TTS_STREAMING=1`); set `TTS_STREAMING=0` for the N2 full-clip path.
- Memory is global (no user/session id), and nothing expires: `recent_turns` keeps the last 10 rows after each prune, overflow goes to `chat_memory` + `rolling_summary` forever. The prune counter resets on restart, so sessions shorter than 10 replies never prune. Option under consideration: archive all `recent_turns` at startup (`prune_recent_turns(keep_last=0)`) so each run starts a fresh chat but keeps long-term recall.
- The mic-to-LLM harness is split across `server/voice_chat.py` (entry/loop), `speechToText.py`, `local_model.py`, `elevenlabs_tts.py`, and `chat_bridge.py` (WS :8766 to the React UI).
- `key_facts` are read by light/full context builders but not auto-written yet (manual / future extractor).

